  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a landscape-scale strategy for reducing nitrous oxide emissions in managed wetland or agricultural waterways by promoting “anammox hotspots.” A canal-and-ditch network runs through vegetated fields, with gates and an ecological valve used to control water flow and create favorable redox conditions. Yellow arrows suggest circulation through channels and ditches, implying that hydrology is engineered to enhance nitrogen transformation at key interfaces.\n\nThe main comparison contrasts strong versus weak anammox. In the strong-anammox case, anammox competes effectively with nitrification and denitrification, converting ammonium and nitrite mainly to nitrogen gas and producing little or no N2O. In the weak-anammox case, shown as a control, nitrogen cycling appears to favor pathways associated with greater N2O formation. The conceptual nitrogen-cycle diagrams at the top summarize this shift among ammonium, nitrite, nitrate, NO, N2O, and N2.\n\nAt the bottom, the figure highlights the environmental zones where these processes are expected to occur: soil–water interfaces, soil–groundwater ecotones, and soil–root interfaces. Together, the image argues that bioengineering hydrological and oxic–anoxic boundary conditions can strengthen anammox and mitigate N2O emissions.",
